2026-08-16 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.44
	* src/encrypt.tcl (_EncRandBytes): the PowerShell branch of the random
	source never reached PowerShell. Its script was built in DOUBLE
	QUOTES, and PowerShell uses $ for variables and [] for type literals
	-- both of which Tcl substitutes. The branch died on
	"can't read \"rng\": no such variable" before exec was called, so on
	Windows without /dev/urandom and without twapi, encryption failed with
	a message about a Tcl variable. Now built in braces, with the byte
	count substituted through string map. Found by nagelfar, which had
	been reporting it as three E lines and two unknown commands.

	* tests/new-0.9.4.35.test (rand-1.1b): a test for that branch, which
	no Linux run had ever entered. It forces the backend and checks that
	the failure is about powershell rather than about a Tcl variable --
	counter-checked, it goes red with the old code.

	* src/main.tcl, src/cat.tcl, src/encrypt.tcl: nagelfar inline markers
	for thirteen "Found constant" warnings (dict keys spelled like the
	variables holding their values) and the two optional sha backends.
	make check now prints nothing at all, where it used to print 27 lines
	in which the three real errors were easy to miss.

	Two things about the markers, both learnt the hard way: "##nagelfar
	ignore" covers exactly ONE following line, so a multi-line dict create
	needs "#7"; and inside a switch body the comment is read as a pattern
	("Switch pattern starting with #"), so it belongs before the switch.
	Verified by running nagelfar 1.3.5, and counter-checked with a
	deliberately broken method -- the silent check still reports it.

	* src/cat.tcl (MergeAcroForm, AcroFieldRefs, MergeResourceDicts,
	WarnDuplicateFieldNames): catPdf merges the interactive form instead
	of keeping the first document's. Until now AppendPdf read both
	dictionaries and ended in the comment "How to do this???", and the
	result kept the /AcroForm of the first document alone. Measured on two
	one-field documents: the second document's widget sat on its page,
	fully formed, with /FT, /T, /Rect and /DA -- and the root catalog
	listed one field. pdftk dump_data_fields reported one field where two
	had gone in, qpdf --check was happy, and nothing warned.

	/Fields is now the concatenation of both arrays. /DR is combined per
	sub-dictionary, with pdf1 keeping its object on a name collision,
	because its appearance streams already point at it. /SigFlags is the
	bitwise OR. /DA and /Q are taken from pdf2 only when pdf1 has none.
	When only the appended document has a form, its (already renumbered)
	AcroForm object is adopted into the first catalog.

	/NeedAppearances is not set, per the fork's standing rule: it damages
	digital signatures, and every field type here writes its own
	appearance stream.

	Field names are deliberately NOT made unique. Two fields of the same
	name are one field with one shared value (ISO 32000-1 clause
	12.7.3.2); that is sometimes what the caller wants, and renaming would
	break the /T reference in any JavaScript shipped with the document.
	The collision is reported through ::pdf4tcl::warnings instead.

	* tests/cat-acroform.test: 11 tests -- both documents with a form,
	three documents, only one side with a form, neither side, colliding
	names, and the two things that must not happen (NeedAppearances
	appearing, the page count changing). Counter-checked: with
	MergeAcroForm removed, five go red.

	Not addressed, and unchanged: fonts. The embedded font stream is
	already shared -- DedupObjects folds it, measured 772348 bytes of
	input to 387881 in the merge -- but the font dictionaries around it
	stay duplicated, because renumbering makes their bodies differ. That
	costs a few hundred bytes per font, not the megabyte the handover
	notes assumed.

	* src/main.tcl (TransBackY, drawTextBox): -newyvar reported the Y
	position in internal page coordinates instead of the caller's. Trans
	turns an incoming y into the internal, bottom-up value; anything
	reported back has to make the same trip in reverse, and this did not.
	Under the default -orient 1 a text box starting at y=200 answered
	633.5 where 208.4 was meant; under -orient 0 the 10000 pt height of a
	tall box leaked into the result. The offset was constant at any y,
	which is what identified it: the value returned was
	pageHeight - (y + height). The documented promise -- "the Y position
	after the last rendered line", with an example that draws a line at
	that position -- was never met. New method TransBackY does the inverse
	of Trans for the y axis, and both -newyvar sites use it.

	Found from the outside: pdf4tcllib's text::writeParagraph passes the
	value on as its return value, and a table of contents was the first
	caller anywhere to stack text and need to know where the last
	paragraph ended. No caller in either tree had used the value before,
	which is how this survived from 0.9.4.23 to 0.9.4.43.

	* tests/new-0.9.4.23.test: the test for -newyvar accepted both the
	right answer and the wrong one. At y=700 it asked for "> 0 and < 700";
	the internal value (about 129) satisfies that, and so does anything
	else below the page top. The correct answer is greater than 700, so
	the test could only ever have caught the fix, not the bug. Replaced by
	three tests: one line down from y, a constant step per line, and the
	same in both orientations. Counter-checked -- with the change to
	main.tcl reverted, all three go red.

	* tests/new-0.9.4.23.test: the file carried THREE cleanupTests. It had
	been extended twice and the closing call came along each time.
	cleanupTests resets the counters, so each section was reported as a
	run of its own and everything after the first one started from zero.
	The consequence is worse than untidy: 23-6.1 failed and tests/all.tcl
	still reported "Failed 0". 16 of the file's 28 tests never reached the
	total. One cleanupTests now, at the end -- the file counts 30 tests,
	and the suite goes from 1005 to 1023.

2026-08-15 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.43
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagCheckNesting, TagEffectiveParent): tagBegin
	refuses a structure element whose parent the standard does not allow.
	ISO 32000-1 tables 335 and 337 fix where TR, TH, TD, THead, TBody,
	TFoot, LI, LBody and TOCI may appear; a cell outside a row or a list
	item outside a list used to land in the tree unremarked. No validator
	reports it -- veraPDF checks conformance, not sense -- and no reader
	repairs it: the file passes and a screen reader announces a table with
	no rows. Only the relations the standard fixes without exception are
	checked; P, Span, Figure and the rest stay unrestricted. NonStruct is
	transparent (clause 14.8.4.2), so wrapping a row in one still passes.
	A refused call throws before the element is created and leaves the
	tree and the open-element stack untouched.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagCheckContent, TagEffectiveKids): tagEnd refuses to
	close a container the standard requires to hold something -- a table
	without rows, a list without items, a row without cells. tagBegin
	cannot see this: at that point the element has no children yet. Marked
	content does not count, so a table holding only text is still a table
	without rows. A refused tagEnd leaves the element open, so the missing
	content can be added and the element closed afterwards.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagCheckContent): an element closed without any
	content -- no marked content, no child element and no annotation -- is
	reported in ::pdf4tcl::warnings. Not refused: the standard permits it,
	it just designates nothing. TD and TH are exempt, because a blank cell
	belongs in the tree and a missing one shifts the column mapping. An
	element holding only an annotation is not empty: a Link or a Form
	consists of its /OBJR and never carries an MCID, so checking marked
	content alone would report the attachment 0.9.4.42 made possible.
	* src/fonts.tcl (createFontSpecEnc): an empty subset reached
	MakeTTFSubset and died there with 'can't read "tlist"' -- a crash
	inside the subsetting code, several frames from the mistake. Codepoints
	that are not integers or lie outside 0..0x10FFFF were passed on just as
	silently. Both are refused now, by name.
	* tests/font.test: font-7.3 was called "empty subset allowed" and
	checked only that the message was not the 256 one, so it stayed green
	while the call crashed. It now checks what it says. font-7.4 and 7.5
	for the codepoint check, 7.6 as a guard -- a check that refused
	everything would satisfy the other three just as well.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagEnsureMC, TagUntaggedReport), src/main.tcl
	(Pdfoutcmd, DrawTextAt, Text): content that belongs to neither a
	structure element nor an artifact is counted and reported once, at
	finish. ISO 14289-1 clause 7.1 requires every piece of content to be
	one or the other; nothing enforced it, and nothing else in the
	toolchain notices, because a validator cannot know which operators were
	meant to be content. A warning and not an error -- untagged content is
	legal PDF and a caller may mean it. Only painting operators count
	(ISO 32000-1 table 51): setting a colour or a font outside an element
	is not a defect, and counting those would report every document however
	carefully marked up. Content inside an XObject does not count, since
	the tag on the Do that places it covers the block. New method
	getUntaggedCount for asking before finish.
	* src/tagged.tcl (tagArtifact): works inside an XObject now. An
	artifact never carries an MCID, because nothing refers to it -- no
	parent tree entry, no /Stm in an /MCR. That is what structure inside an
	XObject would need, which is why tagBegin stays refused there. For a
	purely decorative block the artifact is the correct marking.
	* tests/new-0.9.4.43.test: 42 tests, both directions -- eight refused
	arrangements, eleven permitted ones, the two cases proving a refusal
	leaves no trace, ten for the containers, seven for empty elements,
	three for artifacts in XObjects and eleven for leftover content. With
	the checks removed, 29 fail.
	* src/encrypt.tcl (EncryptStreamBody): the ciphertext of an empty
	stream was dropped while /Length below it was set to the ciphertext
	length. [string replace $body $sstart ${send}-1 ...] leaves the string
	untouched when last is first-1, which is exactly the case for a stream
	of zero bytes. The result was "/Length 32" over no bytes at all: every
	reader opens the file, qpdf reports "expected endstream" and recovers
	the length itself. Empty streams arrived with 0.9.4.42, which gives a
	field without an initial value an appearance dictionary whose stream is
	empty -- ISO 19005 6.3.3 asks for the dictionary, not for contents.
	Two features correct on their own and wrong together. Now assembled
	from three ranges instead of replaced in place.
	* src/main.tcl (addForm): a text field with an initial value carried
	/AP twice. 0.9.4.42 added the key for every text field and the older
	line in the -init branch stayed. ISO 32000-1 7.3.7 does not allow a
	duplicated key; both occurrences pointed at the same object, so no
	reader showed anything.
	* tests/encrypt.test, tests/form.test: seven tests for the two above,
	each with a guard that fails when the helper finds nothing -- the first
	version of both passed on an empty result and measured nothing.
	* tools/bump.tcl: --to and --msg work again and unknown arguments end
	the run with rc=2. They used to be dropped without a word, which made
	"--dry-run" -- a switch this tool never had, it is --show -- write
	every file it was meant to leave alone. Makefile and bumprev.txt
	brought in line with what the tool actually accepts.

2026-08-14 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.42
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagAnnotEntries): an annotation is attached to an
	open Form element as well, not only to Link and Annot. ISO 32000-1
	table 337 gives /Form as the structure type of an interactive field and
	PDF/UA clause 7.18.1 wants the widget annotation attached through
	/OBJR. tagBegin accepted Form all along, so a caller who did the right
	thing got an element in the tree, a field that stayed unreachable, and
	the "annotation is not part of the structure tree" warning on top.
	Measured on the pdf4tcllib form demos: check-tagged.py reported twenty
	failures where it now reports none.
	* src/cat.tcl (DedupObjects): pdf4tcl::catPdf folds objects with an
	identical body onto one and renumbers densely. Documents built from the
	same template duplicate everything they share, above all the embedded
	font programs -- measured on two documents of 28961 bytes, each
	embedding FreeSans: 57757 bytes before, 33762 after, with both font
	descriptors pointing at one /FontFile2 object.
	The comparison covers the complete object body, so two objects are
	folded only when nothing distinguishes them; two font subsets that
	merely look alike must stay apart, since their glyph indices need not
	agree. Pages, the catalog and structure elements are never folded.
	Renumbering is dense rather than leaving gaps, because WritePdf writes
	one xref entry per number from 1 to N and cannot skip a hole -- its own
	comment had asked "TBD handle missing objects?".
	* src/cat.tcl (RemapRefs): regsub cannot call a command per match, the
	replacement is text. A first attempt passed a script there and wrote it
	into the document: the font dictionaries came out containing
	"/FontFile2 apply {{mapping num rest} ...". The rewrite walks the
	matches instead.
	* src/main.tcl: a text field without an initial value gets an empty
	appearance stream rather than a dangling reference. _BuildTextAP
	returns the empty string when there is nothing to draw, and testing
	whether the variable existed instead of what it held wrote
	"/AP << /N  0 R >>" into the file. qpdf, pypdf and tools/check-tagged.py
	all accepted that; veraPDF refused to parse the object. Test
	pdfa-ap-1.0 reads the bytes and checks every /N reference names a real
	object number.
	* src/main.tcl: form fields carry an appearance dictionary under -pdfa,
	and it holds only /N. ISO 19005 clause 6.3.3 requires both. A text
	field had no /AP at all -- _BuildTextAP had been producing the stream
	all along, but nobody referenced it, so the object sat in the file
	unused; a check box wrote /D beside /N, which the clause forbids. A
	reader builds the look from /DA and /V either way, which is why it went
	unnoticed: it only surfaced once a form with a check box could get far
	enough through PDF/A validation for these two rules to be reached.
	Without -pdfa the /D appearance is kept, since removing it everywhere
	would change files that were perfectly fine.
	* src/main.tcl, src/options.tcl: new option -markstyle. The appearance
	of a check box or radio button drew its mark with a ZapfDingbats glyph.
	That font is one of the 14 standard fonts and cannot be embedded, while
	PDF/A clause 6.3.5 and PDF/UA clause 7.21.4.1 both require every font
	program to be embedded -- so a single check box made a document
	non-conformant whatever else it did right. Measured beforehand: a
	document with only text fields contains no ZapfDingbats, one with a
	check box does.
	The mark is now two strokes and the radio dot four Bezier segments,
	needing no font. -markstyle auto, the default, uses them where the
	document claims PDF/UA or a level A conformance and keeps the glyph
	otherwise, so nothing that exists today changes appearance; font and
	vector force one or the other. Rendered side by side at 10, 14, 20 and
	30 point, the two are hard to tell apart.
	* 0.9.4.x/demo/demo-pdfa-3a.tcl: new. The other PDF/A demos show
	conformance level B -- archivable but not accessible. This one is
	PDF/A-3a and PDF/UA-1 at once, which is less work than it sounds:
	level A wants a structure tree, a document language and Unicode
	mappings, and tagging provides all three. Shows headings, a numbered
	list, a table with /Scope on the header cells, a tagged link, and the
	rule and footer as artifacts.
	* tools/check-conformance.py: new. Reads each file's XMP, works out
	which profiles it claims -- pdfaid for PDF/A, pdfuaid for PDF/UA -- and
	runs veraPDF against exactly those. Takes files or directories, so a
	whole output directory can be checked at once; --rules lists the failing
	rules rather than only the verdict. A document claiming nothing is
	reported as such rather than failed against a profile it never
	promised, which is what makes the output worth reading: running
	everything against a fixed profile list mostly produces failures that
	were never in question.
	* src/main.tcl (SetupFont): the standard font warning fires for a
	document claiming PDF/UA through "tagged -ua" as well, not only for
	-pdfa. ISO 14289-1 clause 7.21.4.1 requires embedded font programs just
	as ISO 19005 does, so a tagged form built with base-14 fonts failed
	validation on a point pdf4tcl already knew about and kept to itself.
	Tagging alone stays quiet, since it claims nothing.
	* 0.9.4.x/doc/en/TAGGED.md: new section on form fields, including a
	limitation worth knowing before building an accessible form: check
	boxes and radio buttons draw their mark with a ZapfDingbats glyph, and
	a standard font cannot be embedded, so those two field types cannot be
	part of a PDF/UA conformant document today. Measured -- a document with
	only text fields contains no ZapfDingbats, one with a single check box
	does. Drawing the mark with vector operators would remove the
	dependency.
	* tests/tagged.test: tagged-12.20 checks that a field inside a Form
	element gets /OBJR and /StructParent and raises no warning; 12.21
	checks that a field outside any element is still reported.

2026-08-14 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.41
	* src/options.tcl (CheckPdfa), src/main.tcl (CheckPdfaLevelA): -pdfa
	accepts 1a, 2a and 3a. ISO 19005 level A adds tagged PDF, a natural
	language and Unicode mappings on top of level B. pdf4tcl has written
	the Unicode mappings all along -- a ToUnicode CMap for the standard
	fonts since 0.9.4.9 and for CID fonts anyway -- and tagging exists
	since 0.9.4.36, so what was left was to accept the values and to check
	the two things the caller has to supply. Both are checked in finish,
	and a missing one raises an error: -pdfa 3a writes
	pdfaid:conformance A into the XMP packet, and a reader trusts that.
	Same reasoning as the random source in 0.9.4.35 and the colour range
	check in 0.9.4.39 -- a document that cannot be written beats one that
	only says it conforms.
	* src/main.tcl (SetupFont): using one of the 14 standard fonts while
	-pdfa is set is reported in ::pdf4tcl::warnings, once per document.
	Those fonts have no embeddable font program, which every PDF/A level
	requires, so the document cannot validate -- measured before: -pdfa 3b
	with Helvetica produced a file and said nothing, and it failed
	validation for a reason nothing in pdf4tcl had mentioned. A warning
	rather than an error, because the result is a perfectly good PDF and a
	caller may want it for a draft.
	* What is NOT checked, and stays the caller's responsibility: whether
	the tagging is any good. A tree in which every paragraph is /P and
	every heading is /H1 passes this check and tells a reader nothing.
	Nor is it checked that all content is tagged; see the open points in
	0.9.4.x/doc/en/TAGGED.md.
	* src/main.tcl (_BuildXMPStream): a document that is both PDF/A and
	PDF/UA declares the pdfuaid namespace through a pdfaExtension schema.
	ISO 19005 clause 6.6.2.3.1 requires that for every XMP property outside
	the predefined schemas. Measured with veraPDF against a tagged document
	carrying -ua 1: two failures on pdfuaid:part, "XMP property is either
	not predefined, or is not defined in any XMP extension schema" and "XMP
	property does not correspond to type null". So -pdfa and -ua could not
	be combined, which is exactly the combination that gets asked for --
	an archivable and accessible document. The declaration is written only
	when both are set; a plain PDF/UA document does not need it. Same
	construction as the Factur-X extension schema, which had solved the
	same problem for the fx: namespace.
	* tests/facturx.test (facturx-3.5): the test asserted that no warning
	appears under -pdfa 3b while building the document with Helvetica.
	Factur-X is PDF/A-3, so that document could never have validated; the
	test now embeds FreeSans. The other facturx tests only check what
	facturx writes and are unaffected.
	* src/cat.tcl (WritePdf): the binary comment after the header line had
	three bytes above 127 where ISO 19005 clause 6.1.2 requires four. Every
	reader accepts three, so it never showed until a merged document was
	validated as PDF/A: 154 of 155 rules passed and this was the one that
	did not. pdf4tcl's own writer had four all along.
	* src/cat.tcl (ReadPdf, WritePdf, AppendPdf): the header version is
	taken from the inputs instead of a hardcoded 1.4, and merging keeps the
	higher of the two. Merging documents that pdf4tcl had written as 1.7
	used to downgrade the header to 1.4.
	* src/main.tcl (_BuildXMPStream): the PDF/A extension schemas share one
	pdfaExtension:schemas property with one rdf:Bag. Two namespaces can
	need declaring -- fx: for Factur-X and pdfuaid: when the document is
	PDF/UA as well -- and emitting a block for each produced a second
	property. XMP allows it once, so veraPDF stopped with "Duplicate
	property or field node 'pdfaExtension:schemas'" before validating
	anything. It surfaced the moment examples/facturx.tcl became tagged,
	because that is the first document needing both.
	* examples/tagged.tcl: now built with -pdfa 3a as well, so the demo is
	archivable and accessible at the same time. Level A wants exactly what
	tagging already provides, so the two go together naturally.
	* examples/facturx.tcl: the invoice is tagged and built as PDF/A-3a
	instead of 3b. An invoice is a document that has to be read, and the
	European Accessibility Act aims at this kind of document; level A costs
	nothing here beyond marking up the content, since the font is embedded
	and the metadata are set anyway. The two drawing helpers take a
	structure type, so the layout is untouched: headings are H1 and H2, the
	item table is a Table with TH carrying /Scope and TD below it, and the
	horizontal rules are artifacts rather than content a screen reader
	would announce.
	* tests/pdfa.test: twelve tests added for the a levels, the font
	warning and the PDF/A plus PDF/UA combination.

2026-08-13 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.40
	* src/cat.tcl (MergeStructure): pdf4tcl::catPdf merges the logical
	structure of tagged input documents. Since 0.9.4.36 it dropped the
	structure and warned, because both documents number their pages from
	zero and a naive merge left pages resolving to the wrong elements.
	AppendPdf already renumbers every object of the appended document, so
	its structure tree arrives intact and only needs joining: the
	/Document children of the second root are appended to the first root's
	/K with their /P redirected, the parent tree keys of the second
	document are shifted by the first document's /ParentTreeNextKey in the
	tree and in every /StructParents on a page and /StructParent on an
	annotation, and the two /Nums arrays are merged and sorted, since
	ISO 32000-1 clause 7.9.7 requires increasing keys.
	* src/cat.tcl: MCIDs are deliberately not renumbered. They are scoped
	to the content stream of one page, and merging documents does not merge
	pages, so an MCID of 0 in one document never meets an MCID of 0 in the
	other; only the parent tree keys have to be unique. Measured on two
	two-page documents, both using keys 0 and 1 beforehand: the result has
	four pages with keys 0 to 3, two /Document subtrees under one root, and
	tools/check-tagged.py reports the content of both documents. Chaining
	three documents gives six pages with keys 0 to 5.
	* src/cat.tcl: mixing tagged and untagged input cannot be merged and is
	reported in ::pdf4tcl::warnings, in both directions. Appending an
	untagged document to a tagged one leaves its pages outside the tree;
	appending a tagged document to an untagged one drops its structure,
	because adopting it would leave the first document's pages outside
	instead, which is the same half state and harder to notice. Both
	results are valid PDF and neither is PDF/UA conformant.
	* src/cat.tcl (RenumberDict): the reference mapping was accepted as an
	argument and then not passed on to RenumberRef, so the redirection of
	the appended document's Pages object was silently skipped for the
	trailer, root and info dictionaries. It never showed because AppendPdf
	rebuilds the Pages object afterwards.
	* Two properties of catPdf that predate the structure merge are now in
	the documentation, because they matter once the result is meant to be
	PDF/UA: the catalog of the first document is kept, so the merged file
	carries its dc:title and a reader announces the wrong one -- measured,
	merging "Teil 1" and "Teil 2" gives a document titled "Teil 1" -- and
	embedded fonts are not shared, so two 24729 byte documents merge into
	49296 bytes with two /FontFile2 objects.
	* tests/tagged.test: tagged-9.1 asserted that catPdf removes the
	structure and now asserts the merge; five tests added for chaining,
	the two mixed cases, two untagged documents and the shifting of
	annotation keys. 57 tests, all green. With the merge disabled, ten
	assertions fail.

2026-08-13 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.39
	* src/color.tcl: new. GetColor, the color setters, setBgColor and the
	RGB/CMYK conversions moved here from src/main.tcl and
	src/helpers.tcl. ::pdf4tcl::rgb2Cmyk and ::pdf4tcl::cmyk2Rgb keep their
	namespace, so they remain part of the documented API and stay
	overridable. Concatenated after src/main.tcl, like encrypt and tagged,
	and without a "variable" declaration -- oo::define variable replaces the
	class variable list rather than extending it.
	* src/color.tcl (CheckColorRange): color components are checked against
	0.0 to 1.0. The comment "Maybe range check them here..." had stood in
	the old code twice. Measured before: setFillColor 5 -2 0.5 wrote
	"5 -2 0.5 rg", 1.5 1.5 1.5 wrote itself, and with -cmyk 1 the result was
	"0 1.4 0.9 -4 k". ISO 32000-1 clause 8.6.4 requires 0 to 1 for these
	operands, but out of range values are not a syntax error: readers clamp
	them silently, qpdf --check reported nothing, pypdf read the file, and
	the document simply had the wrong color. It now raises an error, on the
	same reasoning as the random source in 0.9.4.35 -- a document that
	cannot be written beats one that only looks right.
	* src/color.tcl: the 147 standard X11 and CSS color names are resolved
	from a built-in table. winfo rgb needs Tk *and* a display; measured,
	setFillColor red failed under tclsh without Tk and under wish without
	DISPLAY, which rules it out on a server. The table was generated with
	winfo rgb against Tk 8.6.14 rather than typed, and test color-name-1.3
	compares every one of the 147 values against winfo rgb. Names outside
	the table still ask Tk, so nothing that worked before stopped working.
	* src/main.tcl: gradients use the same color pipeline as everything
	else. The separate parser _colorToRGB knew eight names plus hex, so a
	gradient accepted strictly less than setFillColor did in the same
	document: measured, setFillColor {0 0.5 1 0} worked while
	linearGradient with the same list and with "navy" both failed.
	_colorToRGB is gone, both gradients call GetColor, and the shading
	declares /DeviceCMYK instead of a fixed /DeviceRGB when the document is
	CMYK. The components are written through ::pdf4tcl::Nf like every other
	number, so "/C0 [1.0 0.0 0.0]" now reads "/C0 [1 0 0]"; the value is
	unchanged and the old spelling was the odd one out. Tests
	colortorgb-1.1 to 1.4 and 1.7 in tests/new-0.9.4.13.test were updated
	accordingly, with the reason recorded in the file.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagAnnotUnattached): an annotation created while no
	Link or Annot element is open is reported in ::pdf4tcl::warnings. This
	was the one remaining quiet failure in the tagged path: the link works
	when clicked, no error is raised, and assistive technology never
	reaches it, so PDF/UA rule 7.18 cannot be met. A warning rather than an
	error, because an untagged annotation is legal PDF and existing code may
	rely on it, and reported once per document so a page full of links does
	not bury the message. Nothing is inferred -- guessing which paragraph a
	link belongs to would be wrong as often as right.
	* 0.9.4.x/doc/en/pdf4tcl-graphics-and-colors.md: new section on the four
	input forms, the range check, names without Tk and the gradient
	pipeline. Corrected the section on setBgColor, which claimed it set a
	page background. It sets the color of the box that "text -bg" draws
	behind a string and is used nowhere else; measured, setBgColor 1 1 0
	with text -bg 1 produces a rectangle the size of the text. There is no
	method for a page background.

