2026-08-13 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.37
	* src/tagged.tcl: annotations can now be part of the logical structure.
	tagBegin accepts the types Link and Annot; an annotation created while
	such an element is open receives a /StructParent key and the element an
	/OBJR entry pointing at it (ISO 32000-1 clause 14.7.4.4). Without this
	a link works when clicked but is invisible to assistive technology,
	and PDF/UA rule 7.18 cannot be met at all. Nothing is inferred: an
	annotation created outside a Link element stays unattached, because
	guessing which paragraph a link belongs to would be wrong as often as
	right. tools/check-tagged.py reports the unattached case.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagAnnotEntries): link annotations also receive
	/Contents, taken from the element's -alt when the annotation does not
	already carry one. An explicit /Contents in the annotation always wins.
	/Contents is what a reader announces for a link, so PDF/UA requires it
	even though /Alt on the element says the same thing.
	* src/main.tcl (AddAnnot): new. All eight places that created
	annotation objects went through AddObject directly and were therefore
	invisible to the tagging module; they now go through AddAnnot, which
	inserts the extra dictionary entries before the object is written and
	registers it afterwards. A new kind of annotation added later has to
	use AddAnnot as well, or it will be missing from the structure tree
	without any error.
	* src/tagged.tcl: the parent tree now numbers pages and annotations
	from one counter. A page maps to an array of structure elements, an
	annotation to a single element (clause 14.7.4.4), and the entries are
	merged and sorted before writing, because the keys of a number tree
	must appear in increasing order (clause 7.9.7).
	* src/tagged.tcl: new attributes. -listnumbering on L writes
	/A <</O /List /ListNumbering ...>>, so a reader announces the list
	style instead of reading the painted bullet glyph. -id names a table
	cell and -headers lists the -id values of the header cells that apply
	to it; -headers is what works for irregular tables, where -scope alone
	cannot express the relation (ISO 14289-1 clause 7.5).
	* src/main.tcl, src/tagged.tcl: a page carrying annotations is written
	with /Tabs /S instead of /Tabs /R while tagging is enabled, so that
	tabbing follows the structure tree (ISO 14289-1 clause 7.18.3).
	Untagged documents keep /R, which is the row order a plain form wants.
	This only surfaced once examples/tagged.tcl gained its first
	annotation; before that no tagged page had one.
	* examples/tagged.tcl: coordinates corrected. pdf4tcl defaults to
	-orient 1, where y counts downward from the top margin and the margins
	are already included, but the file placed content as if the origin were
	at the bottom left. Measured: the heading sat 12 points above the
	bottom edge, the running foot at the top, and the page read from bottom
	to top, while x was off by the margin because it was added twice.
	veraPDF called that file PDF/UA-1 compliant, since it validates the
	structure tree and not the layout.
	* src/encrypt.tcl (_MD5): the padding used a literal NUL character
	inside a quoted string rather than the escape \x00. Tcl reads either
	form the same way, but the raw byte made grep classify pdf4tcl.tcl and
	src/encrypt.tcl as binary, so "make checkdoc" compared the manual page
	against an empty list of source methods and reported nothing.
	* tools/check-tagged.py: checks that every annotation reachable from a
	page is claimed by exactly one /OBJR, that its /StructParent resolves
	back to that same element, and that link annotations carry /Contents.
	* tests/tagged.test: 47 tests, nine of them new.
	* examples/tagged.tcl: the demo now contains a tagged link, a numbered
	list with /ListNumbering and header cells with /ID.

2026-08-12 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.36
	* src/tagged.tcl: new. Tagged PDF, i.e. the logical structure of ISO
	32000-1 clause 14.7/14.8. Without it a PDF records only where glyphs
	are painted; nothing says which run is a heading, a table cell or a
	page number, and the reading order is whatever order the content
	stream happens to use. New methods: tagged (enable, -lang, -ua),
	tagBegin/tagEnd (open and close a structure element, with -alt,
	-actualtext, -title, -lang, -scope), tagText (both around a single
	text call) and tagArtifact/tagArtifactEnd for content that is not part
	of the document, such as running heads and rules. Structure types are
	restricted to the standard ones of Table 333-337; anything else would
	need a /RoleMap entry and is refused rather than written out and
	silently ignored by readers. The file reopens the class with a second
	oo::define, the way src/encrypt.tcl does, and deliberately declares no
	class variables: oo::define variable replaces the variable list rather
	than extending it, so declaring only pdf there would hide options,
	fonts and images from every method of the class.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagTextString): /Alt and the other text strings are
	written as UTF-16BE literal strings with octal escapes. Not as hex
	strings, because EncryptStringsInBody only rewrites (...) literals and
	a hex string would stay in the clear in an encrypted document. Not via
	QuoteString either, which transliterates above U+00FF and replaces the
	rest with "?" -- acceptable for a bookmark title, not for the only
	text a screen reader gets.
	* src/tagged.tcl (TagEnsureMC): marked content is opened at the first
	painting operation and only for the innermost open element. Opening it
	in tagBegin instead put a container's MCID around its children's;
	veraPDF reported 21 "Nested MCID" warnings against examples/tagged.tcl
	for that. A grouping element such as /L or /TR paints nothing itself
	and must carry no marked content at all. An element interrupted by a
	nested one simply gets a second /MCR when painting resumes.
	* src/tagged.tcl (tagArtifact): closes the open marked content first,
	so an artifact next to an open element sits between two sequences of
	it rather than inside one. ISO 14289-1 clause 7.1 forbids both
	directions; a running foot on the second page of a paragraph spanning
	the page break triggered both rules.
	* src/main.tcl: seven hook calls, each returning at once when tagging
	is off. startPage and endPage manage the per page state and write
	/StructParents; finish writes /StructTreeRoot, /MarkInfo, /Lang and
	the structure objects; Pdfoutcmd and BeginTextObj drive TagEnsureMC.
	The hook in startPage must come after the Flush: Flush writes
	uncompressed while endPage deflates the remaining buffer and derives
	/Length from it, so writing there first put raw bytes in front of the
	deflate stream. The page then inflated to nothing and every reader
	showed it as empty, without an error anywhere. Covered by test
	tagged-8.1a.
	* src/main.tcl (_BuildXMPStream): writes the PDF/UA identification
	schema (pdfuaid:part) when the caller passed -ua to tagged. Opt-in on
	purpose: the entry asserts conformance that pdf4tcl cannot verify --
	embedded fonts, a document title, complete tagging and a sane heading
	order are the caller's responsibility, and a file that claims PDF/UA
	and then fails validation is worse than one that claims nothing.
	* src/cat.tcl (StripStructure): new. catPdf removes the logical
	structure of tagged inputs and appends a note to ::pdf4tcl::warnings.
	Merging structure trees is not a matter of renumbering objects: every
	page carries a /StructParents key indexing the parent tree of its own
	document, and every document numbers its pages from 0. AppendPdf keeps
	the first catalog, so the merge claimed to be tagged while the second
	document's pages pointed into the first document's parent tree.
	Measured on two one-page documents: two pages both carrying
	/StructParents 0, a tree holding only the first document's heading,
	the second document's content orphaned, no error raised. A file that
	lies about its structure is worse than one that has none, because a
	screen reader trusts /MarkInfo and reads the wrong tree instead of
	falling back to the paint order. The orphaned StructElem objects stay
	in the file; unreferenced they are inert, and removing them would mean
	renumbering everything again.
	* src/cat.tcl (WritePdf): the last WriteCh passed "po" instead of
	"pos". upvar creates the variable silently and incr initialises it to
	0, so the position counter was updated in a variable nobody reads.
	Harmless as the final call, but a data error the moment a line is
	added below it.
	* tests/tagged.test: new, 37 tests. Among them tagged-8.1a, which
	inflates the content streams rather than comparing /Length values --
	the first version of that test passed on the broken code as well.
	* tools/check-tagged.py: new. Walks the structure tree, reconstructs
	for every element the text its marked content actually paints, and
	checks BDC/EMC balance, MCID numbering, nesting, /StructParents
	uniqueness across pages, parent tree coverage and orphaned sequences.
	The nesting and uniqueness checks were added after the fact: the first
	version verified everything per page and passed both the nested MCIDs
	and the broken catPdf merge.
	* examples/tagged.tcl: new. Headings, list, table with /Scope, figure
	with /Alt, artifacts and a paragraph spanning a page break. Embeds
	FreeSans.ttf, sets a document title and DisplayDocTitle, and writes
	beside the script rather than into the current directory. Validates
	with veraPDF 1.28.2 as PDF/UA-1: 106 rules and 1357 checks passed,
	none failed.
	* TAGGED.md: new. Documentation, the measurements above and the open
	items -- no /Link with /OBJR for annotations, no /ListNumbering, no
	/Headers//ID for irregular tables, no tagging inside XObjects, and
	nothing that enforces that content is tagged at all.

2026-07-20 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.35
	* src/helpers.tcl (QuoteString): now safe for the binary PDF stream.
	Codepoints above U+00FF are transliterated where a sensible ASCII
	equivalent exists (::pdf4tcl::asciiMap: dashes, quotes, ellipsis,
	arrows, comparisons, euro, trademark) and replaced with "?" otherwise;
	control characters are dropped. Before, such a character did not fail
	here but in finish, as "expected code point values below 0xff but value
	at byte offset N was 0x...", far from its origin. Affects all 18 call
	sites, among them hyperlinkAdd, addAnnotNote and addAnnotHighlight,
	which aborted the document; bookmarkAdd and metadata were already
	guarded by SafeQuoteString, which is now a plain alias.
	* src/helpers.tcl (QuoteStringWarn): appends one note per kind to
	::pdf4tcl::warnings, the package's existing list of non-fatal warnings.
	Nothing is written to stderr. Only control characters are reported;
	transliteration is ordinary prose and stays quiet.
	* src/encrypt.tcl (_EncRandBytes, _InitRandBackend): cryptographic
	sources only -- /dev/urandom, twapi::random_bytes, PowerShell
	RandomNumberGenerator -- and an error when none is available. The old
	fallback to expr rand() produced the AES file key, the initialisation
	vectors and the salts from a 31 bit linear congruential generator seeded
	from the clock: an AES-256 key with at most 31 bits of entropy,
	predictable when the time of creation is known, on every system without
	/dev/urandom, i.e. always on Windows, and without a word to the caller.
	Chosen source in ::pdf4tcl::_randBackend.
	* src/main.tcl (getSubstCount): new method. Reports how many characters
	CleanText could not represent in the font encoding and replaced with
	"?" -- the PDF stays valid, the text does not. Exact from Tcl 9.0 on;
	under 8.6 encoding convertto replaces silently and pdf4tcl cannot see it.
	* tests/new-0.9.4.34.test: new, 48 tests for the addForm extensions of
	0.9.4.30 .. 0.9.4.34 (-align, -color/-borderwidth/-bordercolor/-bgcolor,
	-calculate, -format, -js), which had no test file of their own. Records
	that -bordercolor without -borderwidth emits neither /MK nor /BS.
	* tests/new-0.9.4.35.test: new, 36 tests for the above.
	* pdf4tcl.man: getSubstCount, ::pdf4tcl::_randBackend, extended
	description of ::pdf4tcl::warnings, and a note at _md5Backend that all
	three backends compute the same MD5.

